@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization

@*
    The header build chip, gated to signed-in users.

    🚨 The gate belongs HERE rather than at the wiring site: PortalLayoutBase renders the HeaderLinks
    slot OUTSIDE its AuthorizeView (alongside the theme toggle, which is deliberately public), so
    anything placed in that slot is served to anonymous visitors — and public pages exist. Build
    version and pod name are deployment detail: they fingerprint the instance for a visitor with no
    reason to know it, and they answer a question only a signed-in user is asking.

    It is a wrapper around a separate component rather than an @if inside one so that the gate covers
    the READ as well as the markup — an unrendered child never initialises, so an anonymous page load
    (a crawler on a public Store page, say) never opens a System-impersonated read of the Admin
    partition just to decide it has nothing to show.
*@
<AuthorizeView>
    <Authorized>
        <PlatformUpdateChipView />
    </Authorized>
</AuthorizeView>
